td-busd interop corpus
======================

Two bodies of evidence about a D-Bus implementation this crate has to agree
with, and neither is this crate's own reading of the specification:

  *.conversation   bytes a real client and the reference `dbus-daemon`
                   actually exchanged, replayed by ../src/recorded.rs and
                   recorded by ../examples/dbus-capture.rs
  auth/            dbus's OWN auth-script suite, vendored from its source
                   tree, replayed by ../src/authscript.rs


Why this exists beside the hand-laid corpus
-------------------------------------------

`src/corpus.rs` holds encodings laid out by hand from the specification, and
they are what proves this crate agrees with the specification AS THE AUTHOR
READ IT. A misreading passes those tests too, because the same reading wrote
both the code and the fixture.

Recordings are the other kind of evidence. Nobody wrote down what the reply
should be; the reference implementation did, and the client on the other side
is the one td has to interoperate with. The two kinds fail differently and
neither replaces the other:

  hand-laid   covers what SHOULD happen, including refusals no real client
              ever sends, and can be written before the code exists
  recorded    covers what DOES happen, including the spellings, pipelining and
              framing real clients use that nobody would think to invent

What a recording does NOT carry is upstream's INTENT. It is one session
between one client and one daemon version; where it and the specification
disagree, the specification wins and the recording is evidence about a
particular implementation.

`auth/` is the third kind and the one that carries intent: dbus's own tests for
its own server, stating what the reference implementation is REQUIRED to
answer — including to peers no client would ever be. They are the only fixtures
here that neither this author nor a running daemon produced.

  hand-laid   what SHOULD happen, as the author read §D
  recorded    what DOES happen, between real programs
  auth/       what upstream REQUIRES, in upstream's own words

The third kind paid for itself on arrival. The hand-laid corpus asserted that
`AUTH EXTERNAL willy` is answered `REJECTED`; `invalid-hex-encoding.auth-script`
says `ERROR`, and upstream is right, and so is the specification: an argument that
cannot be READ claims no identity, so no identity was refused, and ERROR is
what the ERROR Command section reserves for a peer that "did not understand the
arguments to the command", after which the sender "must continue as if the
command causing the ERROR had never been received".

The specification is not of one mind, and the honest version says so: its
server state table calls REJECTED the answer when "the client failed to
authenticate or there was an ERROR IN RESP", which reads the other way. dbus
settles its own tension in code and in this fixture — `process_data` decodes
before the mechanism is ever consulted — and interoperating means answering
what the reference implementation answers.

So it is a conformance difference and not a better outcome for the client:
libdbus answers this ERROR with CANCEL and disconnects on anything but
REJECTED, and answers `REJECTED EXTERNAL` by finding it has no mechanism left
to try and disconnecting too. Either way the connection ends.

The misreading wrote the code and the fixture alike, which is exactly the
failure the hand-laid corpus cannot detect on its own.


Format
------

One `.conversation` file per recorded session. Line-oriented, ASCII:

    # comment                    ignored
    name: <case>                 required, once
    note: <text>                 optional, once
    C <hex>                      one read's worth of bytes from the CLIENT
    S <hex>                      one read's worth of bytes from the DAEMON

Frames appear in the order they crossed. **Frame boundaries are data, not
formatting** — each `C`/`S` line is exactly what one `read(2)` returned, and
the boundaries are the part real clients get interestingly wrong:

  * libdbus sends the leading NUL and `AUTH EXTERNAL <hex>` in ONE write, then
    `BEGIN\r\n` and its first message in ANOTHER — so a handshake that
    consumed its whole buffer would swallow the `Hello` and the peer would
    look like one that connected and never spoke.
  * sd-bus (busctl) sends `\0AUTH EXTERNAL\r\nDATA\r\nNEGOTIATE_UNIX_FD\r\n
    BEGIN\r\n` as a SINGLE write, before reading any reply at all. One `feed`
    of that must produce three replies and stop at `BEGIN`.

A reader that normalized frames away would lose both cases.


Provenance
----------

  Reference daemon:  D-Bus Message Bus Daemon 1.15.8
                     /gnu/store/01dmmnlj3mqc6cmms83fkayzz9gbk2yh-dbus-1.15.8
  Recorded as:       uid 1001, on the build host, against a private session bus
                     started with `dbus-daemon --session --print-address --fork`
  Recorder:          ../examples/dbus-capture.rs (this tree)

  libdbus-listnames.conversation
    client:  dbus-send 1.15.8 (libdbus)
    command: dbus-send --session --dest=org.freedesktop.DBus --type=method_call
             --print-reply /org/freedesktop/DBus org.freedesktop.DBus.ListNames
    covers:  the specified transcript; Hello pipelined with BEGIN; a method
             call and its reply; the unsolicited NameAcquired signal

  libdbus-introspect.conversation
    client:  dbus-send 1.15.8 (libdbus)
    command: as above, member org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable.Introspect
    covers:  a reply whose body is one large string, several KiB

  libdbus-addmatch.conversation
    client:  dbus-send 1.15.8 (libdbus)
    command: dbus-send --session --dest=org.freedesktop.DBus --type=method_call
             --print-reply /org/freedesktop/DBus org.freedesktop.DBus.AddMatch
             "string:type='signal',interface='org.freedesktop.DBus',\
member='NameOwnerChanged'"
    covers:  a match rule with the escaping §D's parser has to handle

  libdbus-becomemonitor.conversation
    client:  dbus-monitor 1.15.8 (libdbus)
    command: dbus-monitor --address unix:path=<socket>
    covers:  BecomeMonitor — which §D REFUSES, so this is a recording of
             traffic td-busd must reject rather than serve. Note dbus-monitor
             1.15.8 does NOT call AddMatch: it passes an empty rule array to
             BecomeMonitor, which is why AddMatch above is a separate file

  sdbus-busctl.conversation
    client:  busctl, elogind 255 (sd-bus)
    command: busctl --address=unix:path=<socket> list --no-pager
    covers:  the empty `AUTH EXTERNAL` + `DATA` spelling, and the entire
             handshake pipelined into one write

These are recordings of protocol traffic, not copied dbus source: no file at
the top level is derived from dbus's own source tree. The traffic was produced
by running the programs above, which are packaged by Guix at the store path
named.


Provenance — auth/
------------------

  Upstream:   dbus 1.16.2
  Source:     https://dbus.freedesktop.org/releases/dbus/dbus-1.16.2.tar.xz
  SHA-256:    0ba2a1a4b16afe7bceb2c07e9ce99a8c2c3508e5dec290dbb643384bd6beb7e2
  Path:       dbus-1.16.2/test/data/auth/  (copied byte-for-byte)
  Semantics:  dbus-1.16.2/test/internals/dbus-auth-script.c, which is the
              driver that defines every directive below
  License:    AFL-2.1 OR GPL-2.0-or-later, at the recipient's choice. The
              scripts carry no per-file SPDX marker, and `.reuse/dep5` — which
              exists, with one stanza, for `subprojects/*.wrap` — has no entry
              covering them,
              so they take the project default stated in dbus's COPYING; the
              driver that gives them meaning carries that identifier
              explicitly. Vendored as test DATA, as `td-txt/spec` vendors GNU
              grep's and GNU sed's.

  How the URL and the hash were tied together. The file these 15 were copied
  out of arrived named `dbus_1.16.2.orig.tar.xz`, which is Debian's name for a
  source package's upstream tarball rather than upstream's own. The two are the
  same bytes, and that is checked rather than assumed: this host's store holds
  Guix's `builtin:download` fixed-output derivation for that release,

    /gnu/store/qk85ia65s49kkh7i0pr4gwx0n8vyd8zb-dbus-1.16.2.tar.xz.drv

  whose `url` is the Source line above and whose `sha256` is the SHA-256 above,
  character for character. So the pin is upstream's URL and upstream's hash,
  and the tarball on hand is that release under another name.

  Selection rule: every `*.auth-script` in that directory whose side marker
  line is `SERVER`. That is 15 of the 19 files. The other 4 are `CLIENT`
  scripts — they drive the peer half of the handshake, and td-busd has no
  client role. The rule is mechanical, so re-running it against the pinned
  tarball reproduces this directory exactly; `every_committed_script_parses_as_
  a_server_script` refuses a CLIENT script that arrived here by hand.

  Per file, so that "pristine" is checkable and not merely asserted. A count
  says how many files are here, not which one each is; `sha256sum -c` against
  these says both. The replay requires the 15 to be distinct from each other,
  which catches a substitution, and these catch an edit.

    9d12c68986274cf0ac6d55c905939be6bda96e48d264fdc5ec09c44419a7764f  anonymous-server-successful.auth-script
    f71f482849c7aaacc27261801b150437e222b5a7b0593d9e2af24287f66b583c  cancel.auth-script
    9c57c239408d198e2ab73b9073069c5083927c123bc4e70b93fefc04395ff0c9  cookie-sha1-username.auth-script
    bc571e9463b1c68a2648ddc7bca5397b33d3a91227860504eee677df6a492416  cookie-sha1.auth-script
    bbddfc64227a76d2af51d3dce5796aeec48825fe447613a96b72436cf88aa961  external-auto.auth-script
    9f31fcddd62bd0caae804f16037b444c9e742d10a62d41c43a2e27c577cf0fe5  external-failed.auth-script
    68b8d2311d3ed2d521f868654c1744f5137aae2df01ec4c0f4d0c511ea14d3a4  external-root.auth-script
    c33dbe7eca0a96b7367af5a7f1498bcc82eec91d2953f8e76acfcb2b70afa5fe  external-silly.auth-script
    f9bd6e51ca74695572ef98d6a656bfcb41161d54bfeed57cd47e5c4aec5456c8  external-successful.auth-script
    2338a45a1a2cc1b1a0d82dadb53d7949183109c3751ab7fba6136d96ffec9758  external-username.auth-script
    074d78ea9dd98215470188bb211d215e05694617a501a3814649274ff6c3e8e8  extra-bytes.auth-script
    e2af3c07a29aa8b7e781191acc303bc6524ad4e4af1b72e7048587cb7e023f60  fail-after-n-attempts.auth-script
    0633217f36b9050f61241139c79bd37f72faea7bddafe95a4d57b6a150ac96f3  invalid-command.auth-script
    a1ed54e7a4b1c3bfca11ff55f5bb80c3cd8ecbbee49d23e803d1f2d9ececb5bf  invalid-hex-encoding.auth-script
    34a27f59f54f99589742819251a8597fc264176415d95efb93cf834e25c8af01  mechanisms.auth-script

  Files are PRISTINE. Nothing is edited to make it pass, and nothing is edited
  to make it run: where td answers differently, the difference is recorded in
  `DEVIATIONS` in ../src/authscript.rs with its reason, and the script is still
  replayed and still required to fail.

  An exemption is not a hole. Each one pins the SUBSTRING its failure must
  contain, so a divergence that starts failing for some other reason — a parse
  bug, a disconnect, the right refusal at the wrong step — reds as loudly as
  one that starts passing. Both were measured: with only "it still fails"
  required, a broker that began accepting ANONYMOUS replayed clean, because
  `anonymous-server-successful` then fails one step later than it used to and
  one failure looked like any other.

  Of the 15: 10 pass and 5 are recorded deviations — two DBUS_COOKIE_SHA1 and
  one ANONYMOUS, mechanisms §D refuses by name, and two that open with
  `NO_CREDENTIALS`, which this crate has no identity for. On the only transport
  it serves SO_PEERCRED always answers; a peer it could not answer for has to
  be refused before a handshake exists, which is the transport's job and not
  built yet (UNSAFE.md surface #10).

  All five are REPLAYED. An earlier draft skipped the last two by name and
  checked their excuse against the fact that the script still said
  NO_CREDENTIALS — a property of the file, which never changes, so teaching the
  crate the very thing the excuse is about would have left them skipped
  forever. The refusal to model an absent credential is now itself the recorded
  failure, and stops being one the day it stops being true.


  The one thing the scripts assume that this crate does not
  ---------------------------------------------------------

  No script sends the connection's leading NUL. Upstream's `DBusAuth` never
  sees it: the transport takes the credentials byte off the stream first, and
  hands the auth object a stream that starts at the first command. This crate
  puts that byte inside the handshake instead — it is the one place a peer's
  very first write is checked — so the replay supplies it once, ahead of the
  first `SEND`. It is in the replay and not in the data, and
  `../src/authscript.rs` says so where it happens.

  The other one is the peer. Upstream's NO_/ROOT_/SILLY_CREDENTIALS set the
  socket credential a claim is checked against; td wants each script replayed
  against a peer whose claim and credential are DIFFERENT numbers, or a broker
  recording whatever the peer claimed would pass all fifteen. Both are had by
  replaying every script TWICE and requiring the same verdict: once faithfully,
  where the kernel says exactly what the script says, and once through a
  mapping onto a sentinel credential no script names. The faithful run is why
  ROOT_CREDENTIALS still means a peer the kernel calls root rather than a
  literal in an otherwise ordinary script.


  The one line read differently from upstream's own driver
  ---------------------------------------------------------

  `AUTHENTICATED_WITH_UNUSED_BYTES` is not a `DBusAuthState`. It appears in one
  script and nowhere else in dbus, and `auth_state_from_string` resolves it by
  PREFIX match against `AUTHENTICATED` — so upstream's driver reads
  extra-bytes' two `EXPECT_STATE` lines as the same state, and the distinction
  its author plainly meant to draw is not enforced by the thing that runs it.

  Read here at face value instead: the first line requires bytes to be left
  over and the second requires none. Strictly stronger, and the reason the
  `consumed` contract has a state check as well as an `EXPECT_UNUSED`. The cost
  is that a future script using the spelling loosely would red where upstream
  passes — a failure that needs reading rather than believing, which is the
  right way round.

  It is the only directive read DIFFERENTLY, which is not the same as the only
  one upstream handles and this does not. `WAITING_FOR_MEMORY`,
  `HAVE_BYTES_TO_SEND`, `ALLOWED_MECHS` and `WIN_ONLY` are refused rather than
  interpreted. No committed script reaches them, and one that did would fail
  loudly rather than quietly become a shorter script that passes.


Descriptor passing
------------------

`dbus-capture` forwards bytes and cannot forward SCM_RIGHTS: doing so needs
`UNSAFE.md` surface #10, which this crate does not have. A recording of a
conversation that passed a descriptor would therefore hold a message whose
UNIX_FDS field describes descriptors the bytes do not carry.

Two guards, because the first one is a judgement call and the second is not:

  1. the recorder REFUSES to write a conversation in which `AGREE_UNIX_FD`
     was seen, unless `--allow-fds` says the operator has checked it;
  2. `src/recorded.rs` decodes every recorded message and asserts that none
     declares a descriptor — mechanical, in the crate where the decoder lives,
     and not dependent on the operator having been right.

Every file here was recorded with `--allow-fds` (all five negotiate it; none
passes a descriptor), and guard 2 is what actually holds the claim — guard 1
only makes an operator look. Guard 2 is also stricter than it appears:
`decode_from_client(bytes, 0)` refuses any message declaring a descriptor
before the assertion is reached, so the assertion states the property rather
than being the only thing enforcing it.


Re-recording
------------

    cargo build --manifest-path td-busd/Cargo.toml --example dbus-capture
    dbus-daemon --session --print-address --fork      # note the unix:path=...

    td-busd/target/debug/examples/dbus-capture \
        --listen /tmp/td-capture.sock \
        --upstream <the daemon's socket path> \
        --name <case> --note <what it covers> --allow-fds \
        --out td-busd/spec/<case>.conversation &

    <client> --address unix:path=/tmp/td-capture.sock ...

The recorder stops on a half-second silence (`--idle-ms`) rather than on EOF,
so a client that holds its connection open — `dbus-monitor` does — still
produces a file. It gives up waiting for a client after ten seconds
(`--accept-ms`); the usual cause is a client `--address` that does not name
the recorder's socket.

A re-recording against a different daemon gets a different GUID, and one made
by a different user states a different uid. Both are read out of the recording
itself, so the replay does not need updating — except for `RECORDER_UID` in
../src/recorded.rs, which is the fallback for the `DATA` spelling, where the
client states no identity for the reader to find.

Note what the replay does NOT take from the recording: the credential. The
peer is built MAPPED, claiming the recorded uid but credentialed as a sentinel
no recording contains, so that "charged to the credential" and "charged to the
claim" are different answers. Built the obvious way they would coincide, and
the corpus would not notice a broker that recorded whatever the client said.
The GUID gets the same treatment in a second replay.

Adding a file here means adding it to `RECORDINGS` in ../src/recorded.rs. A
file nothing replays is worse than no file, and `the_corpus_is_whole` reads
this directory and compares it against that list, so an unlisted file reds.


Not yet done
------------

  * The auth scripts cover the HANDSHAKE alone. dbus's `test/data/` also
    carries message-level fixtures — `invalid-messages/*.message-raw` among
    them — which belong beside `corpus.rs`'s decoder cases. Not vendored here:
    that is a second selection rule, with its own reading of a second driver,
    and it is a separate landing rather than a wider one.
  * A recording is replayed by DIRECTION, not by interleaving: swapping a
    client and a daemon frame in a file would not be noticed. Causal order is
    guaranteed by the recorder instead, which logs a frame before forwarding
    it, so a reply can never be recorded ahead of the request that caused it.
  * Recordings of td-busd ITSELF answering a real client. That needs the
    socket, which needs surface #10. Until then a recording is replayed
    through `Handshake::feed` and the decoder directly, which covers the
    parsing and the replies but not the transport.
  * Routing, names and match rules (rung 14). When those land the recordings
    grow into whole call/reply exchanges, and this corpus is the shape they
    go in.
